e not a communist state  so that's another example and i i could  go on i think central bank digital  currency is a good example  the chinese have pioneered this idea and  suddenly everybody's saying  where's the fed's central bank digital  currency where's the bank of england the  bank of england has set up a task force  to explore  this idea so across the board i think  we're seeing the osmosis of of cold war  in  in cold war too and what's particularly  fascinating about that neil ferguson's  interviews he said  we then we realized we could get away  with it and i remember writing a piece  in february last year about china's  we're hand locked down thinking this  could never happen  in the uk but then of course it happened  all across the west  when it comes to the security versus  liberty  tension do you think that a lot of the  west's are leaning towards security in  the last year especially on covet  it's because of this sort of state of  emergency that we've been in this global  pandemic scaring people into this  and so after the threat is over maybe  we'll be less like china in that way  well in do i try to argue that it wasn't  a straightforward choice between  letting the virus rip and getting to  herd immunity  as it were naturally and locking  everything down  that was the wrong choice uh to think  about that there was a choice  it existed in in january which was to do  what the taiwanese did and the south  koreans did  uh they didn't lock down in fact taiwan  has never had  a lockdown what they did was to ramp up  testing because it was possible to  test for the new virus very early on  once it had been  uh genetically sequenced uh at the same  time  to create a system of contact tracing uh  and then to  make be able to isolate or quarantine  people who are suspected of being ill  they they did that  very quickly uh and as a result hardly  anybody in taiwan has died of  of covert 19 i think the grand total is  11  to date despite the fact that they're  right next door  to the people's republic so i think one  obvious question for public health  officials in the west is  why didn't you do that why did you sit  around  january february and into mid-march  really not doing very much at all  and then in mid-march you read neil  ferguson's report panicked at the  thought that there were going to be  2.2 million deaths in the u.s half a  million in the uk  and and then you shut everything down in  this very indiscriminate way  that had lots of unintended consequences  the full scale of which i don't think we  we understand even even today so i i  think one has to realize that there were  other options  but we decided and i think it was a  wrong decision to copy  the people's republic of china rather  than to copy the republic of china  taiwan if we'd copied the taiwanese  playbook  we would have had i'm absolutely sure  significantly less  excess mortality and much much less  economic disruption  now you could say uh contact tracing  apps on our phones that sounds like  that sounds like a terrible threat to  civil liberties but i can assure you  it's a  it's a smaller threat than being under  house arrest as a great many of us were  for a large part of  of last year i mean if if if it's civil  liberty to be  to be shut in your own home under a  shelter-in-place order  then uh as ian hislock once said i'm a  banana  although taiwan does shut down its  borders pretty early on because it was  suspicious of the people's republic of  china i mean do you think that's  an acceptable trade-off to make  throughout history and  times of plague there have been  restrictions on travel  and one of the strangest things about  2020 was  how slow the us and the uk were  to realize that uh it was clearly  not wrong for donald trump to argue back  in january that there should be a travel  ban that there should be limits  on passengers coming into the us from  china but  uh he came up with the idea a couple of  weeks too late for really to make much  impact and of course there were  exceptions so u.s citizens and green  card holders were exempt the same is  true by the way  today indians can't travel  freely to the united states but indians  with green cards can  and u.s citizens who've been in india  can and that isn't going to stop the  indian variant from  arriving in the united states so yeah i  mean the taiwanese response which of  course at its counterparts in australia  and new zealand and other countries  was the right one when there's a new  pathogen running a mark in a country  stop people coming from that country  regardless of what passport or visa they  have  that's that's the kind of knowledge that  they already had about plagues back in  the middle ages  and let's talk about the cold war um you  you mentioned your book that cold war ii  has  already started and and you argue that  china  is the aggressor to this from from what  i what i've been talking to people with  in china and  whether it's academics or people they  actually think that is the west  who are itching to start a cold war too  what do you say to those people  well as a visiting professor at xinhua i  have quite a few  chinese academic friends and and friends  in  the world of chinese policy i've been  struck by how readily  they've accepted the cold war ii analogy  uh there's not a lot of pushback when  you when you make  this kind of argument uh of course as in  the first cold war  uh the communist side always argues that  it was the west that started it uh but  but this is a pretty unconvincing  argument i think when you look back on  xi jinping's track record since he  he came to power in china because really  from that moment on china's been far  more  explicit in its geopolitical and  technological  ambitions uh and as i try and show in  the book  discussions within uh the chinese  intelligentsia  uh are really quite explicit about the  objective of displacing  the united states as the dominant world  empire  uh with the people's republic of china  uh when you think about the way in which  china has increased its uh national  security expenditures its capabilities  uh its militarization of the south china  sea it's increasing assertiveness  towards  uh other asian countries think of the  way it's been beaten  it's been beating up on australia  recently it's kind of hard to claim that  that cold war ii started uh in  washington as opposed to  to beijing and i think we're rather in  the us in the same situation that we  were in at the beginning of  of cold war one in in the 1940s when  george orwell and winston churchill said  we're in a cold war most americans were  very slow to  accept that there was a sort of denial  that that persisted  almost until the outbreak of the korean  war in 1950  and i think we're still at that stage  now in cold war too a lot of  intellectuals  at stanford or harvard would say no no  this isn't cold war  ii that's the wrong analogy we should be  striving for co-opetition  with china which is a hideous word i  think this is the stage of denial that  you typically find in the west at the  beginning of a cold war well i guess  part of their worries is probably that  you know in a cold war as we saw in in  the 60s  you know we had people on both sides  kind of characterizing  each other as these uh one-sided  one-dimensional  players and actors and in many ways  china is not quite like the ussr it's  not ideologically driven in the same way  that the ussr was it seems much more  keen on lining its pockets  uh wherever possible um so do you think  that  the cold war mentality you know sorry  the cold war parallel  has limitations in how to understand  china as the new  strategic threat well i think it's  true to say that in cold war one there  was a great deal of  uh of caricaturing of the other side but  that wasn't  by any means the worst thing about one  the nuclear brinkmanship and the proxy  wars that cost  enormous numbers of lives from southeast  asia to  to central america and sub-saharan  africa  i think you can have a cold war without  those things certainly without the  nuclear brinkmanship and the  proxy wars but i think it's almost  inevitable that in the way  things are currently going that the  rhetoric on both sides  is becoming more and more hostile  and attitudes towards the chinese  government uh are becoming more and more  negative  uh in in the western world the pew group  showed that with some remarkable survey  data  back in october but my argument for cold  war ii  is not an argument against  a happy and cordial relationship i just  don't think a happy and cordial  relationship is possible  with this chinese government pursuing  these strategic goals  the thing we're trying to avoid is hot  war three i mean we do not want the us  and china to end up in a shooting war  whether it's over taiwan  uh or the south china sea or some other  issue  and the great point of cold war one was  to avoid  world war three it was to avoid a  nuclear exchange between the united  states and the soviet union and it did  that  contained the soviets expansion pretty  peacefully in the sense that there  wasn't a  head-to-head war over uh europe uh and  and i think that's really the point of  cold war that you'd rather have a cold  war than a hot war and i think that's  that's why cold wars happen and why  ultimately they're preferable  to making a series of miscalculations  which i think we're still well capable  of doing  that produce world war three it's not  difficult to imagine  a major escalation happening over an  issue like taiwan  and if you talk to people of the us  military i think of my  old friend jim stavridis who's just  published a novel about this  the scenarios are pretty terrifying  because the u.s china war would very  quickly escalate  from the taiwan strait to bombing of  mainland targets and then potentially to  nuclear exchanges that's that's how  steve reedus imagines it in his novel so  as i've been arguing now for more than  two years um  it you'd rather have cold war ii than  world war three  okay so you say that we should compete  with china but not copy it  so what does that competition look like  i think it looks like  what we currently see in the realm of  vaccines  where the west is able to take advantage  of its technological  edge and the greater competition that  there is when it comes to vaccine  development in the western world i mean  the competition between companies  biotech companies like moderna  and beyond tech is one of the reasons we  ended up with mrna  vaccines i think it looks like that in  artificial intelligence  one of the striking things about ai is  that the us still has a  qualitative lead partly because the best  chinese  researchers in ai end up working in the  united states and often stay  and ultimately become citizens so i  think this is a technological contest  i think cold war ii will be much more uh  a race  uh in technology than cold war one was  of course there was a  tech race a space race uh in in  cold war one and i think there will be  in cold war ii and a cyberspace  race and right now the us leads in i  think  most of the key domains but it will not  continue to lead  unless it really does uh maintain that  level of  investment in research and development  and competition which is really the key  to producing the best results uh and so  we mustn't make the mistake of thinking  that the way to compete with china  is through economic planning and by my  account we now have three plans coming  from the biden administration  uh in the form of major legislation huge  amounts of expenditure  total bill of around six trillion  dollars and i look at that and i ask are  we about to repeat the mistakes  of the 1960s and 70s when it seemed as  if  the only way to compete with the soviet  union was to do economic planning  the way they did that turned out to be a  completely  wrong conclusion and the way the united  states won the cold war was in fact with  with a free market economic model under  ronald reagan uh not with the kind of  thing john kenneth gobraith was arguing  for in the 1960s  well actually i'm intrigued that you  brought up chinese researchers and  scientists in ai for example  as you know in in america and in the  west in general there's been a greater  suspicion of chinese  nationals or ethnic chinese working  in stem stem areas where do you think  what do you what role do you think they  play in this sort of future competition  between the two great powers or should  they be  welcomed uh into the west or do you  think that we should be  careful being careful about what they  can take back to china  well this is a huge issue in a way that  it wasn't in  in cold war one there were relatively  few uh  soviet citizens in the united states at  any point  uh in the cold war that were uh in the  later part of the cold war exchanges but  they were on a tiny scale compared with  the really huge amounts  of exchange that there is between china  and the u.s  hundreds of thousands of chinese  students until recently at american  universities and often studying  stem subjects a really significant  number of graduate students working in  in key areas such as artificial  intelligence or quantum computing  and it's true to say uh and this is  something that that we know from  uh from the fbi that there has been  espionage as a consequence  we know that the chinese government uses  all kinds of means to try  to hack western technology  uh breakthroughs often through cyber  means but also through  human intelligence and this creates an  enormous  problem because of course if if one  chinese national working at one big tech  company turns out to be a spy  then suspicion falls on the others and  unfortunately that can include  uh american citizens uh often  uh people from chinese uh of chinese  heritage who've lived in the united  states for  many years uh so i do think we 